"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 12. The surgical findings indicate a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 3), pathological stage pT3b, positive surgical margins, and invasion of the seminal vesicles. Importantly, there was no lymph node metastasis documented. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy difference is noted as the Gleason pattern and ISUP report are missing from the biopsy. The nodal status is documented as no lymph node metastasis.\n\nCountervailing findings include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and no lymph node metastasis. However, the positive surgical margins and extraprostatic extension contribute to the risk profile. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding the subsequent trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its subsequent trend is crucial for refining this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 38.08 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 30.47–45.70 months; its coverage has not been validated."
